/// Allocates uninitialized storage for `count` elements. A negative count, an overflow of `count * stride(T)` or an
/// allocation failure Aborts. A zero-byte request allocates nothing and returns a nonnull address aligned for `T`.
public func allocate<T>(count: isize) -> raw/T
/// Releases storage that `allocate` returned, at its start address and without destroying any element; null and
/// zero-byte results are ignored. Every pointer derived from the allocation becomes invalid.
public unsafe func release<T>(storage: raw/T)
/// Moves `value` into live, aligned, writable storage that holds no initialized value, destroying nothing.
public unsafe func initialize<T>(storage: raw/T, value: T)
/// Forms a shared Slice of `length` elements starting at `storage`; the caller fixes the result Origin `s`. When `length` is
/// positive, `storage` is nonnull and aligned for completed contiguous storage, which is not written for `s`. Formation
/// reads no element; the programmer must write valid values before reading any noinit elements through this view.
public unsafe func slice<T>(storage: raw/T, length: isize) -> Slice<T> during s
